In article <dpapp.759544548@amisk.cs.ualberta.ca>, dpapp@amisk.cs.ualberta.ca (Denis R. Papp) writes:
>Also, whats this I hear about mixing potions?  How? 

You can dip one potion into another and get something different.
It's mostly a useless feature, because you seldom get anything
useful out of it.

Here are the details:

If you dip something into water, none of this applies.

There is a 1/10 chance of them blowing up and you losing 1-10 hp.
If one is healing or extra healing, and the other is sickness/
hallucination/blindness/confusion, you get one potion of water.
If you dip a diluted potion you get water (?).  Otherwise you get 
one of the following:
  - water (1/8)
  - sickness (diluted?) (2/8)
  - A random (diluted?) potion (1/8)
  - Nothing (4/8)

The potion you dip into is used up.

All this info may not be exactly right, but the bottom line is that
you lose one potion, and seldom get anything useful.  A wand of 
polymorph is a much better way to turn some useless potions into
something useful.
